Adding Fano to xet-core as compression scheme 3
===============================================

This is the whole change, against xet-core main 7af65ba2. It adds one variant to
`CompressionScheme` and one dependency. Nothing else in the store moves: the chunk record is
unchanged, the chunk hash is still taken over the uncompressed bytes so deduplication is untouched,
and `serialize_chunk` already falls back to `None` when a scheme does not make the chunk smaller.

The crate carries the uncompressed length inside its own payload as a varint, so
`decompress_from_slice(&self, data: &[u8])` keeps its current signature. That was deliberate: the
alternative was threading a length parameter through three call sites, and it costs three bytes on
a 64 KiB chunk.

--- a/xet_core_structures/Cargo.toml
+++ b/xet_core_structures/Cargo.toml
@@
 [dependencies]
+fano = "0.1"

--- a/xet_core_structures/src/xorb_object/compression_scheme.rs
+++ b/xet_core_structures/src/xorb_object/compression_scheme.rs
@@ -22,10 +22,11 @@
 /// Dis-allow the value of ascii capital letters as valid CompressionScheme, 65-90
 #[repr(u8)]
 #[derive(Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Clone, Copy, Default)]
 pub enum CompressionScheme {
     None = 0,
     LZ4 = 1,
     ByteGrouping4LZ4 = 2, // 4 byte groups
+    Fano = 3,             // byte-plane order-0 entropy coding, for tensor data
     #[default]
     Auto = 99,
 }

@@ impl From<&CompressionScheme> for &str
             CompressionScheme::Auto => "auto",
             CompressionScheme::LZ4 => "lz4",
             CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4 => "bg4-lz4",
+            CompressionScheme::Fano => "fano",
             CompressionScheme::None => "none",

@@ impl TryFrom<u8> for CompressionScheme
             0 => Ok(CompressionScheme::None),
             1 => Ok(CompressionScheme::LZ4),
             2 => Ok(CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4),
+            3 => Ok(CompressionScheme::Fano),
             99 => Ok(CompressionScheme::Auto),

@@ impl FromStr for CompressionScheme
             "none" => Ok(CompressionScheme::None),
             "lz4" => Ok(CompressionScheme::LZ4),
             "bg4-lz4" => Ok(CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4),
+            "fano" => Ok(CompressionScheme::Fano),

@@ pub fn compress_from_slice
             CompressionScheme::None => data.into(),
             CompressionScheme::LZ4 => lz4_compress_from_slice(data).map(Cow::from)?,
             CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4 => bg4_lz4_compress_from_slice(data).map(Cow::from)?,
+            // returns the input unchanged when the scheme does not pay; serialize_chunk then
+            // stores the chunk as None, exactly as it already does for a too-large LZ4 result
+            CompressionScheme::Fano => match fano::compress(data) {
+                Some(v) => Cow::from(v),
+                None => data.into(),
+            },

@@ pub fn decompress_from_slice
             CompressionScheme::None => data.into(),
             CompressionScheme::LZ4 => lz4_decompress_from_slice(data).map(Cow::from)?,
             CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4 => bg4_lz4_decompress_from_slice(data).map(Cow::from)?,
+            CompressionScheme::PlaneEntropy => fano::decompress(data)
+                .map(Cow::from)
+                .map_err(|e| CoreError::MalformedData(e.to_string()))?,

@@ pub fn decompress_from_reader
             CompressionScheme::None => copy(reader, writer)?,
             CompressionScheme::LZ4 => lz4_decompress_from_reader(reader, writer)?,
             CompressionScheme::ByteGrouping4LZ4 => bg4_lz4_decompress_from_reader(reader, writer)?,
+            CompressionScheme::Fano => {
+                let mut buf = Vec::new();
+                reader.read_to_end(&mut buf)?;
+                let out = fano::decompress(&buf)
+                    .map_err(|e| CoreError::MalformedData(e.to_string()))?;
+                writer.write_all(&out)?;
+                out.len() as u64
+            },


Choosing the scheme
-------------------

`choose_from_data` currently runs BG4Predictor and picks `ByteGrouping4LZ4` when the maximum KL
divergence of the four byte-position histograms exceeds 0.02, and `LZ4` otherwise. Two policies,
both measured:

  Cheapest change, and the one to ship first. Where the predictor already says bg4 — F32 and BF16
  tensors — use Fano instead. That is exactly the set where it wins on all three axes:
  11-36% smaller, 1.4-2.4x faster to compress, 1.4-2.1x faster to read.

      if bg4_recommended { CompressionScheme::Fano } else { CompressionScheme::LZ4 }

  Full coverage. Also try it where the predictor says LZ4 and the chunk is not text-like. F16, FP8,
  INT8, INT32 and UINT32 tensors all fail the KL test, which is why they are stored raw today, and
  the coder takes 4-42% off them. The trial costs one encode, 50-75 us per 64 KiB chunk, and the
  result is kept only if it beats the alternative by the margin the store chooses. A threshold near
  8% keeps quantised GGUF raw, where the 6% saving is not worth the read cost.

A predictor that avoids the trial encode is the obvious next step: the encoder already computes a
Miller-Madow order-0 estimate of every plane to choose its plane count, and exposing that as
`pe_estimate_bits(src, n)` would give the selector an expected size for one histogram pass.


Rolling it out
--------------

`XorbChunkHeader::validate` rejects an unknown scheme byte, so a client without the decoder cannot
read a scheme-3 chunk. The order is therefore:

  1. ship the decoder in hf_xet and in every server-side path that reconstructs chunks;
  2. only then enable scheme 3 in the writer, or enable it server-side at rest and serve decoded
     bytes to clients that predate the decoder — the reconstruction path already reads every chunk
     it serves.

The header `version` byte stays 0: the record layout does not change, only the scheme table.


What it costs at run time
-------------------------

Per 64 KiB chunk on one core, medians of at least three interleaved runs:

  class                          today (bg4-lz4 or raw)      fano
  F32 compress / read            72-124 us / 23-30 us        52-57 us / 13-22 us
  BF16 compress / read           126-137 us / 27-31 us       56-58 us / 18 us
  F16, FP8, INT8 (raw today)     33-55 us / 3-9 us           63-90 us / 21-56 us

Encoder scratch is thread-local and grows once per thread to one segment; the decoder writes only
into the caller's buffer.
